window.__IDX__=[{"p":"tx-cryo","t":"Cryolipolysis (Fat Freezing)","g":"tx","s":"Protocol · Slimming & Body Contouring","k":"Cryolipolysis (Fat Freezing) 4D Cryo · CoolSculpting · 3D Cryo · Fat Freeze A non-invasive fat reduction treatment that uses controlled cooling to freeze and permanently destroy fat cells in a targeted area, without damaging skin, nerves or surrounding tissue. Controlled cooling to −5°C to −10°C triggers apoptosis (natural cell death) in adipocytes. The destroyed fat cells are cleared by the lymphatic system over 4–12 weeks. This reduces the localised fat bulge permanently — it is body contouring, not weight loss. Localised, pinchable subcutaneous fat pockets Upper and lower abdomen Flanks / love handles Inner and outer thighs (saddle bags) Back fat / bra bulge Submental fat (double chin) Banana roll (under-buttock fold) Upper arm fat Cryoglobulinaemia, cold agglutinin disease, paroxysmal cold haemoglobinuria Raynaud's disease or known cold hypersensitivity / cold urticaria Pregnancy or breastfeeding Active skin infection, open wounds or rashes in the treatment area Hernia in the treatment area Implanted devices (pacemaker, metal implants) near the treatment area Severe varicose veins in the treatment area Neuropathy or loss of sensation in the treatment area Recent surgery within 6 months in the treatment area Chilblains or history of perniosis Active cancer or malignancy Uncontrolled diabetes (high or unstable blood sugar) BMI above 35 — doctor assessment required, limited efficacy Pinch fold under 2 cm — zone not suitable, document and inform doctor History of keloid or hypertrophic scarring Recent aesthetic injectable in the area within 4 weeks Clients on anticoagulants — bruising risk −5°C to −10°C (machine controlled) Per machine protocol — typically 35–50 min per probe As per treatment plan; 1 gel membrane = maximum 2 probes 2 cm fat fold — mandatory pinch test before every probe 2 minutes, mandatory Verify client identity & consent Check client ID against appointment. Confirm signed Cryo consent form is on file. Check medical-condition stickers on the client record card. BP & blood sugar check Check BP and blood sugar for hypertensive, diabetic and GLP-1 programme clients. Record in the Daily Vitals Register. Room & machine preparation Room clean, bed co"},{"p":"tx-nsls","t":"NSLS — Non-Surgical Liposuction (Cavitation + RF)","g":"tx","s":"Protocol · Slimming & Body Contouring","k":"NSLS — Non-Surgical Liposuction (Cavitation + RF) Ultrasound Cavitation + Radiofrequency · Non-Surgical Lipo A two-stage non-invasive body contouring treatment. Ultrasound cavitation ruptures fat cell membranes; radiofrequency then tightens the skin over the treated area in the same session. Cavitation uses 40 kHz ultrasound to create micro-bubbles in the fluid around adipocytes. The bubbles implode, rupturing the fat cell membrane without damaging nerves, vessels or connective tissue. Released fatty acids and glycerol are cleared through the lymphatic system. RF then delivers controlled electrical energy that heats the dermis to 40–45°C, contracting existing collagen immediately and stimulating new collagen and elastin over weeks. Localised stubborn fat that does not respond to diet and exercise Abdomen, flanks and love handles Thighs (inner and outer) Upper arms Upper and lower back / bra fat Buttocks contouring Gynaecomastia (male chest) — low intensity Double chin (1 MHz head only) Facial contouring and jowls — RF ONLY Pacemaker, cardiac defibrillator or any implanted electronic stimulation device Metal implants, surgical plates, pins, screws or rods in or near the area Cochlear implants or any active implanted medical device IUD in place — do not treat abdomen / pelvic area Pregnancy (any trimester), suspected pregnancy, or breastfeeding Active cancer, on chemotherapy or radiotherapy, or cancer history in the treatment area DVT, pulmonary embolism or active clotting disorder Severe uncontrolled hypertension (systolic above 180 mmHg) Severe uncontrolled heart disease or cardiac event within 6 months Active liver disease or liver failure — the body cannot process released lipids Active kidney disease or renal failure Active epilepsy or sound/vibration-triggered seizures Active autoimmune conditions: Lupus, Scleroderma, Dermatomyositis Active severe skin infection, open wounds, ulcers or acute inflammation in the area Active herpes outbreak in the treatment area Blood-borne disease with active liver involvement Any surgery in the treatment area within 3 months; liposuction within 6 months Any filler or fat-dissolving injectable in the area within 4 weeks Under"},{"p":"tx-rfbody","t":"RF Body Tightening & Contouring","g":"tx","s":"Protocol · Slimming & Body Contouring","k":"RF Body Tightening & Contouring Radiofrequency Skin Tightening · Non-Surgical Tuck · RF Sculpting Controlled deep heating of the dermis and subcutaneous tissue to tighten loose skin, soften cellulite and improve post-weight-loss laxity. Tissue resistance to the RF current generates heat. At 40–43°C existing collagen fibres contract immediately, and a wound-healing response drives new collagen and elastin production over the following weeks. Skin laxity and sagging Cellulite Post-weight-loss loose skin Tummy, arms, thighs, hips, love handles, upper back Calf reshaping Breast lift (B-Lift) — non-surgical Face and chin contouring (low power) Pregnancy or lactation Cardiac pacemaker or implanted electronic device Metal implants, orthopaedic plates, screws or fixators in the treatment area Copper IUD (abdominal treatment) Active cancer or malignancy Active skin infection or open wounds in the area Recent surgery in the area within 3 months Peripheral neuropathy — cannot report heat accurately Uncontrolled diabetes History of keloid scarring Very thin or fragile skin Prior radiation to the area 40–43°C Hold 40–43°C for 10–15 minutes per zone Check temperature every 2 minutes Above 44°C — lift probe immediately Ultrasound gel or pure cosmetic glycerin for high-thermal work 10–15 W only Pre-execution checklist Verify no metal implants, plates, screws or fixators. Confirm no copper IUD for abdominal work. Remove all jewellery and piercings. Confirm no active skin conditions, burns or recent procedures. Confirm no pacemaker. Coupling medium Apply the recommended coupling medium evenly — ultrasound gel for thermal RF, or pure glycerin where the gel evaporates too fast under high heat. Handpiece motion Move continuously in rapid, overlapping circular motions. Build heat gradually to 40–43°C. Therapeutic window Hold 40–43°C for 10–15 minutes per zone. This is the window that remodels collagen. Temperature monitoring Check every 2 minutes with the built-in thermal sensor or an external IR thermometer. Thermal burn prevention Above 44°C: lift the probe away, increase handpiece speed, apply cooling gel. Do NOT continue at elevated temperature. Post-treatment Remove gel, apply s"},{"p":"tx-hifubody","t":"HIFU Body","g":"tx","s":"Protocol · Slimming & Body Contouring","k":"HIFU Body High-Intensity Focused Ultrasound — Body Focused ultrasound energy delivered at fixed depths to disrupt deep structural fat and tighten the fascial (SMAS) layer without breaking the skin. Ultrasound energy is focused at a precise depth, raising tissue temperature at that focal point to a coagulation threshold while leaving the skin surface untouched. This destroys deep adipocytes and triggers fascial contraction and collagen remodelling. Deep structural fat that does not respond to cavitation Abdomen and tummy Jawline, arms, thighs Fascial (SMAS) tightening on the body Pregnancy or lactation Cardiac pacemaker or implanted electronic device Metal implants in the treatment area Active cancer or malignancy Severe hepatic or renal impairment Hernia in the target area Recent surgery in the area within 3 months Severe varicose veins in the area Active skin infection or open wounds in the area Subcutaneous fat under 2.5 cm — not suitable, document and inform the doctor Keloid history Neuropathy in the area Uncontrolled diabetes 8.0 mm (subcutaneous fat) · 13.0 mm (deep fat / fascial) Parallel linear grids, 5 mm apart 2.5 cm, caliper verified Abdominal midline (linea alba), umbilicus, all major nerve pathways Logged per cartridge per patient in ERP before and after every session Map the treatment area Mark boundaries on the skin before starting. Mark exclusion zones Midline of the abdomen (linea alba), the umbilicus, and all identified major nerve pathways. Cartridge selection 8.0 mm for subcutaneous fat, 13.0 mm for deep fat and fascial layers. NEVER use face cartridges on the body. Shot count check Check and log the remaining shot count on each cartridge in ERP before beginning. Gel application Standard ultrasound gel, full and even coverage. Transducer contact Place firmly, 100% flat and flush. Gaps and air pockets lose energy focus and cause uneven results. Systematic delivery Deliver shots along the pre-mapped grid lines, 5 mm spacing. Work methodically — do not jump or skip zones. Never deliver two consecutive pulses over the same coordinate. Pain management Expect a deep, hot, pricking sensation per shot. Reduce energy if intolerable, but do not drop be"},{"p":"tx-injlipo","t":"Injection Lipolysis (Mesolipo)","g":"tx","s":"Protocol · Slimming & Body Contouring","k":"Injection Lipolysis (Mesolipo) Fat Dissolving Injection · Deoxycholic Acid / Phosphatidylcholine · Mesolipo A prescription injectable that chemically dissolves small, dense pockets of fat that do not respond to device-based treatments. A lipolytic solution is injected in a grid pattern into the subcutaneous fat layer. It disrupts adipocyte membranes, and the released lipids are cleared by the lymphatic system and metabolised by the liver. Local inflammatory swelling for 3–7 days is expected and is part of the mechanism. Submental fat (double chin) Bra fat Lower abdomen Small, dense, stubborn fat pockets not suited to cryo or cavitation Pregnancy or breastfeeding Aspirin, NSAIDs (ibuprofen, naproxen) or blood thinners in the last 72 hours Active skin infection, sores or open wounds in the area Keloid formation history in the treatment zone Active cancer or malignancy Severe hepatic or renal impairment Uncontrolled diabetes Known allergy to any component of the lipolytic solution Anticoagulant therapy — physician clearance Bleeding disorders Very thin overlying skin Prior filler in the same area within 4 weeks 30G or 32G 1.0–1.5 cm; 1.0 cm for submental area 4–6 mm, perpendicular (90°) into subcutaneous fat 0.2 mL Never into muscle or dermis — dermal tracking causes blistering Firm sterile gauze pressure for 2 minutes Pre-execution checklist Confirm no aspirin, NSAIDs or blood thinners for 72 hours. No active infection or open wound. No keloid history. Not pregnant or breastfeeding. Signed injection-specific consent on file. Skin sanitisation Sanitise thoroughly with 70% isopropyl alcohol or chlorhexidine. Allow 30 seconds to dry. Grid mapping Apply a grid transfer tattoo at 1.0–1.5 cm spacing over the marked fat pocket. Do not mark or inject outside the boundary of pinchable subcutaneous fat. Pinch and inject Pinch the fat pouch away from underlying muscle. Insert perpendicular to 4–6 mm depth. Inject 0.2 mL per point. Withdraw swiftly to avoid superficial tracking. Intra-procedure watch Watch for immediate blanching (possible vascular compromise) or extreme sudden pain — either requires immediate cessation and physician evaluation. Post-injection care Firm steri"},{"p":"tx-ems","t":"EMSculpt / EMS Body Toning","g":"tx","s":"Protocol · Slimming & Body Contouring","k":"EMSculpt / EMS Body Toning RF-EMS · Electromagnetic Muscle Stimulation · EMS Belt · NMS High-intensity electromagnetic stimulation that produces supramaximal muscle contractions to build muscle definition and burn fat over the treated muscle group. Focused electromagnetic energy induces involuntary muscle contractions far beyond what voluntary effort can achieve. The muscle responds with hypertrophy, and the metabolic demand drives localised lipolysis in the overlying fat. Abdominal definition and core strengthening Gluteal lifting Arm and thigh toning Post-partum core rehabilitation (with clearance) Adjunct to fat reduction programmes Cardiac pacemaker, defibrillator or implanted electronic device Metal implants, plates, screws or rods in or near the treatment area IUD in place — do not treat the abdomen Pregnancy or breastfeeding Epilepsy or seizure disorder Active cancer or malignancy Recent surgery in the area within 6 months Hernia in the treatment area Severe cardiac or pulmonary disease Active DVT or clotting disorder Uncontrolled hypertension Uncontrolled diabetes Recent abdominal surgery Menstruation — abdominal treatment may be uncomfortable 30 minutes per zone Build progressively to client tolerance across the session Full flat contact with the muscle belly — reposition if the contraction feels off-centre Screen contraindications Screen every client before every session — metal, pacemaker, IUD, pregnancy, epilepsy, hernia, recent surgery. Remove all metal All jewellery, piercings, belts, phones and cards away from the applicator field. Positioning Position the client and secure the applicator flat over the target muscle belly. Ramp intensity Start low. Increase progressively over the first 5 minutes to the maximum comfortable level. Monitor Check comfort every 5 minutes. Contraction should be strong but not painful. Cramping = reduce. Cool down Machine cool-down / tapping phase at the end to reduce lactic soreness. Documentation Log zone, intensity reached, duration and response in ERP. Drink 2 litres of water Adequate protein intake to support muscle recovery Expect soreness like after a hard workout for 24–48 hours No intense workout of the same mus"},{"p":"tx-glp1","t":"GLP-1 Medical Weight Management","g":"tx","s":"Protocol · Slimming & Body Contouring","k":"GLP-1 Medical Weight Management Semaglutide · Tirzepatide (Mounjaro) · Medical Weight Loss Injection A prescription-only pharmacological weight management programme using GLP-1 receptor agonists, delivered under strict clinical oversight with monthly monitoring and structured titration. GLP-1 receptor agonists slow gastric emptying, increase satiety and reduce appetite signalling. Weight loss follows reduced caloric intake. The drug is an adjunct — nutrition, protein intake and resistance training are mandatory parts of the protocol, not optional extras. BMI above 30 Age 27+ with at least one weight-related comorbidity: Type 2 diabetes, hypertension, dyslipidaemia or obstructive sleep apnoea Personal or family history of Medullary Thyroid Carcinoma (MTC) or MEN2 History of acute or chronic pancreatitis Pregnancy, active breastfeeding, or planning pregnancy within 60 days Known hypersensitivity to the drug or excipients Type 1 diabetes (not an indication) Active gallbladder disease Severe gastroparesis or significant GI motility disorder Severe renal impairment — physician decision History of eating disorder — psychological review first Planned surgery or anaesthesia — see the aspiration risk protocol Escalate ONLY if the current dose is well tolerated. Never accelerate the schedule. 2.5 mg weekly × 4 weeks → 5 mg → 7.5 mg → 10 mg → 12.5 mg → 15 mg (varies by individual response) Freeze titration at the current dose for a further 2–4 weeks until symptoms resolve Minimum 60–80 g daily to prevent lean muscle wasting Minimum 150 minutes moderate intensity weekly, including resistance training Cold chain 2–8°C. Temperature log maintained daily. Baseline evaluation Record weight, BMI, blood pressure, waist circumference. Order HbA1c, eGFR and LFTs. Contraindication screen Complete the full screening checklist. Any absolute contraindication stops the process. Informed consent Review GI side effects, out-of-pocket cost, perioperative fasting protocol and long-term maintenance expectations. Initial dose & training Dispense the lowest starter dose. Demonstrate subcutaneous self-injection technique in clinic. Monthly titration review Review at each monthly visit. Titrate u"},{"p":"tx-drainage","t":"Lymphatic Drainage & Whole Body Therapy","g":"tx","s":"Protocol · Slimming & Body Contouring","k":"Lymphatic Drainage & Whole Body Therapy G5 / G10 Drainage · WBT with oil · Manual Lymphatic Drainage · FDS wrap Mechanical and manual drainage that moves disrupted fat and interstitial fluid toward the lymph node clusters so the body can clear it. Non-surgical fat reduction relies entirely on the lymphatic system to carry away disrupted adipocytes. Mechanical percussion, vibration and directed manual strokes accelerate that transport. Without it, mobilised lipids can be reabsorbed. Mandatory support after cavitation, cryolipolysis and HIFU Fluid retention and puffiness Post-treatment soreness Inch-loss maintenance Active DVT or clotting disorder Active infection or fever Active cancer without oncologist clearance Open wounds, ulcers or acute inflammation in the area Pregnancy — abdominal work Severe varicose veins in the area Recent surgery in the area Very fragile or thin skin Severe osteoporosis — reduce percussion intensity Uncontrolled hypertension Always toward the nearest lymph node cluster — groin for lower body, axilla for upper abdomen and torso 5 minutes minimum post-cavitation, cryo and HIFU 45 minutes post-session where prescribed Light to moderate; never over bone, spine or kidney area Post-care cleansing Remove all treatment gels. Apply the soothing post-treatment cream appropriate to the preceding modality. Manual lymphatic drainage 5 minutes of upward strokes toward the nearest node cluster — groin for lower body, axilla for upper abdomen and torso. Mechanical drainage G5 / G10 percussion or vibration over the treated area. Avoid spine, kidney area and bony prominences. FDS application Apply Fat Dissolving Solution belt or wrap for 45 minutes post-session where prescribed. Activity brief Advise 10 minutes light activity immediately, and 20–30 minutes brisk walking within 4–6 hours. Drink 2.5–3 litres of water daily for 4 days 20–30 minutes brisk walking within 4–6 hours of the session Keep to the 72-hour elimination window on alcohol and refined carbohydrate No alcohol for 72 hours No heavy refined carbohydrate for 72 hours Do not skip the cardio — mobilised fatty acids are reabsorbed if not burned Mild bruising from percussion Dizziness during d"},{"p":"tx-lipolaser","t":"Lipo Laser (Low-Level Laser Lipolysis)","g":"tx","s":"Protocol · Slimming & Body Contouring","k":"Lipo Laser (Low-Level Laser Lipolysis) Laser Lipo · Lipo Laser Pads Low-level laser pads placed on the skin that stimulate fat cells to release their stored contents, which are then cleared through the lymphatic system. Low-level laser energy creates transient pores in the adipocyte membrane, allowing triglycerides to move into the interstitial space. Because the fat cell is not destroyed, immediate post-session cardio and lymphatic drainage are essential — otherwise the lipids are simply reabsorbed. Circumferential reduction on abdomen, flanks, thighs and arms Clients who cannot tolerate cavitation or cryo Adjunct within a slimming programme Pregnancy or breastfeeding Active cancer or malignancy Active liver or kidney disease Photosensitivity disorder Active skin infection or open wounds in the area Pacemaker or implanted electronic device [TO CONFIRM against machine IFU] Uncontrolled diabetes Thyroid disorder Clients on photosensitising medication Clients unable to exercise post-session — efficacy will be poor Flat, full skin contact over the target fat pad [TO CONFIRM] per machine IFU 20–30 minutes cardio or G5 drainage immediately after Verify and consent Confirm client ID, session number and signed consent. Cleanse and position Cleanse the area. Position the client comfortably. Pad placement Place pads flat with full skin contact over the target zone. Secure with straps. Run the cycle Run for the machine-specified time. Monitor client comfort. Mandatory drainage / cardio Immediately follow with G5/G10 drainage or 20–30 minutes of cardio. Without this the released lipids are reabsorbed. Documentation Log zone, time, and post-session activity completed in ERP. 20–30 minutes cardio immediately after the session Drink 2.5 litres of water daily Keep to a low-carbohydrate window for 24 hours after Do not skip the post-session activity No alcohol for 48 hours No heavy meal immediately after the session Mild warmth or redness Skin irritation under pads Client unable or unwilling to do post-session cardio Photosensitising medication disclosed Pads placed over broken skin Lipo Laser (pad-based low-level laser) [TO CONFIRM] — no dedicated LIFE SOP supplied; protocol b"},{"p":"tx-wrap","t":"Inch-Loss Wrap & FDS Application","g":"tx","s":"Protocol · Slimming & Body Contouring","k":"Inch-Loss Wrap & FDS Application Papaya Gel Wrap · Fat Dissolving Solution (FDS) · Heat Blanket Therapy A topical enzymatic or thermal wrap applied to the body to draw out fluid, improve skin texture and support the fat-clearance phase of a contouring programme. The active gel is applied under an occlusive wrap, often with gentle heat, which increases local circulation and lymphatic movement. Most of the immediate circumference change is fluid; the lasting benefit comes from the drainage support it gives the primary treatment. Post-cavitation, post-cryo and post-HIFU drainage support Fluid retention and bloating Skin texture and softness Pre-event temporary contouring Pregnancy Active skin infection, open wounds, rash or dermatitis in the area Known allergy to any wrap component Severe cardiovascular disease (heat load) Uncontrolled hypertension (heat blanket) Active DVT Claustrophobia — full body wrap may not be tolerated Diabetes with neuropathy — reduce or omit heat Very fragile skin 45 minutes Moderate setting; never on a client who cannot report heat accurately Required for first-time clients where a new active gel is used Screen and consent Screen allergies and contraindications. Confirm consent. Cleanse Cleanse and dry the area. Apply gel Even layer of the prescribed gel or FDS over the target area. Wrap Apply the occlusive wrap smoothly — firm but never restricting circulation. Check the client can breathe and move comfortably. Heat phase Apply the heat blanket at a moderate setting where prescribed. Check comfort every 10 minutes. Removal and drainage Remove the wrap, wipe residue, follow with G5/G10 or manual drainage. Documentation Log product, area, duration and response in ERP. Drink 2 litres of water after the session Light walking the same day Report any rash or itching that develops after leaving No hot shower for 4 hours No harsh scrub on the area for 24 hours Mild redness after wrap removal Contact allergy / itching rash Any itching or tingling during the wrap Known allergy not screened before application Heat blanket used on a client with neuropathy V1 Heat Blanket · body wrap SOP-SLM-001 §3 (FDS) · [TO CONFIRM] full wrap protocol"},{"p":"tx-carbon","t":"Carbon Laser Peel (Hollywood Facial)","g":"tx","s":"Protocol · Skin & Dermatology","k":"Carbon Laser Peel (Hollywood Facial) Carbon Facial · Hollywood Peel · Skin Carbon Peel A two-pass laser facial that uses a layer of medical-grade liquid carbon as a target for the laser, deep-cleaning pores and resurfacing the skin with zero downtime. Liquid carbon penetrates the pores and binds dead skin, sebum and debris. The 1064 nm Nd:YAG laser is fired at low fluence; the carbon absorbs the energy and vaporises instantly, carrying the debris with it. The thermal effect also warms the dermis, stimulating collagen and tightening pores. Dull skin and lack of glow Oily skin and enlarged pores Superficial acne scars Blackheads and congestion Anti-ageing / collagen stimulation Pre-event instant brightening Active acne with open pustules or cystic acne in the treatment area Active herpes simplex anywhere on the face — even outside the treatment zone Active skin infection, open wounds, ulcers or crusting in the area Active eczema, psoriasis or dermatitis flare in the area Rosacea with active flushing or pustular rosacea Sunburned skin — even mild tan with redness Vitiligo in the treatment area Pregnancy (any trimester) or breastfeeding Active cancer or on chemotherapy / radiotherapy Uncontrolled diabetes Epilepsy or photosensitive seizure history Autoimmune skin conditions: Lupus, Dermatomyositis, Pemphigus Porphyria or any photosensitivity disorder Isotretinoin (Roaccutane) use within the last 6 months Medium or deep chemical peel in the area within 4 weeks Dermal fillers, Botox or injectables in the area within 2 weeks Microneedling, microdermabrasion or dermabrasion within 2 weeks Any surgery in the area within 3 months Laser or IPL in the same area within 4 weeks Waxing, threading or depilation on the face within 48 hours Sunbed or UV tanning within 4 weeks Photosensitising medication: tetracycline, doxycycline, ciprofloxacin, methotrexate St John's Wort High-dose anticoagulants: warfarin, heparin Tattoos or permanent makeup in the treatment area — the laser will darken the ink Under 18 years Known allergy to carbon lotion Unable to give informed consent History of post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation — patch test mandatory History of keloid or hypertrophic scar"},{"p":"tx-qswitch","t":"Q-Switch Nd:YAG — Pigmentation & Skin Toning","g":"tx","s":"Protocol · Skin & Dermatology","k":"Q-Switch Nd:YAG — Pigmentation & Skin Toning Laser Toning · Q-Switch Laser · Laser Pigmentation Treatment Ultra-short, high-energy laser pulses that shatter unwanted pigment into microscopic fragments the body then clears, used for pigmentation, melasma, sun spots and overall skin brightening. The Q-switched mechanism stores energy and releases it in nanosecond bursts. Melanin granules absorb the energy and fracture without heating surrounding tissue. 1064 nm reaches deep dermal pigment and vascular lesions; 532 nm targets superficial pigment and red/orange pigment. The fragments are cleared by the lymphatic and immune system over weeks. Melasma Freckles and sun spots Post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation (PIH) Nevus of Ota Vascular lesions General skin brightening and toning Uneven skin tone Active herpes simplex anywhere on the face Active skin infection, open wounds, ulcers or crusting in the area Active eczema, psoriasis or dermatitis flare in the area Sunburned or recently tanned skin Vitiligo in the treatment area Pregnancy (any trimester) or breastfeeding Active cancer or on chemotherapy / radiotherapy Uncontrolled diabetes Epilepsy or photosensitive seizure history Autoimmune skin conditions: Lupus, Dermatomyositis, Pemphigus Porphyria or photosensitivity disorder Isotretinoin within the last 6 months Chemical peel in the area within 4 weeks Fillers, Botox or injectables in the area within 2 weeks Microneedling or dermabrasion within 2 weeks Laser or IPL in the same area within 6 weeks Photosensitising medication: tetracycline, doxycycline, ciprofloxacin, methotrexate High-dose anticoagulants Under 18 years Unable to give informed consent Fitzpatrick V–VI for 532 nm — high PIH risk; use 1064 nm at low fluence only History of PIH — patch test mandatory History of keloid or hypertrophic scarring Thyroid disorder or hormonal imbalance driving pigmentation — melasma may recur PCOS with active hormonal melasma History of gold therapy — permanent discolouration risk (chrysiasis) Oral contraceptives or HRT — hormonal pigmentation, manage expectations Peripheral neuropathy in the area Nevus of Ota and vascular lesions — dermatologist clearance mandatory before sta"},{"p":"tx-tattoo","t":"Q-Switch Tattoo Removal","g":"tx","s":"Protocol · Skin & Dermatology","k":"Q-Switch Tattoo Removal Laser Tattoo Removal Laser fragmentation of tattoo ink so the body's immune system can gradually clear it, delivered over a course of widely spaced sessions. Nanosecond pulses shatter ink particles into fragments small enough for macrophages to carry away through the lymphatic system. Clearance happens between sessions, not during them — which is why the 6–8 week interval is non-negotiable. Black and dark ink tattoos (1064 nm) Coloured tattoos — red, orange, yellow (532 nm) Partial fading before a cover-up Amateur or traumatic tattoos Pregnancy or breastfeeding Active infection or open wound over the tattoo Sunburned or recently tanned skin over the area Isotretinoin within the last 6 months Active cancer or on chemotherapy Photosensitivity disorder or photosensitising medication Keloid formation over the tattoo Under 18 years Fitzpatrick V–VI — high PIH risk, 1064 nm and low fluence only Unknown ink composition — risk of paradoxical darkening; test spot mandatory Cosmetic / permanent makeup ink — high paradoxical darkening risk, dermatologist decision Keloid history elsewhere Diabetes — slower healing 1064 nm for black / dark ink · 532 nm for red, orange, yellow 4–8 J/cm² 3–6 J/cm² 2–6 mm Minimum 6–8 weeks between sessions Topical anaesthetic 30–45 minutes before, removed completely before firing Verify and consent Confirm written consent for each specific tattoo. Photograph the tattoo with a scale marker before session 1 and every 3 sessions. Assess the ink Colour, density, depth, professional vs amateur, age of tattoo, any prior removal attempts. Test spot Mandatory on the first session. Fire 1–2 pulses. Wait 60 seconds. Assess frosting response. Blistering = stop. Numbing Topical anaesthetic 30–45 minutes prior. Remove completely before firing. Eye protection Certified laser goggles on client and operator. Delivery Even single-pass coverage, 10–20% overlap. Never stack pulses. Expect immediate white frosting over the ink. Immediate cooling Cold compress 10–15 minutes. Dressing If blistering occurs: antibiotic ointment and non-stick dressing. Change daily. Documentation Wavelength, fluence, spot size, pulse count, response, photographs"},{"p":"tx-peel","t":"Chemical Peels","g":"tx","s":"Protocol · Skin & Dermatology","k":"Chemical Peels Glycolic · Salicylic · Mandelic · Lactic · TCA · Yellow · Black · Jessner's · Pyruvic · Arginine · Phenol Controlled application of an exfoliating acid to trigger the skin's healing cascade at a chosen depth — brightening, resurfacing, treating pigmentation and acne. The acid causes a controlled injury to a predetermined depth. The skin responds with desquamation of damaged cells, accelerated cellular turnover and collagen remodelling. Depth is controlled by acid type, concentration, pH, number of layers, contact time, skin prep and prior retinoid use. Dull skin and uneven texture Active acne and oily skin Hyperpigmentation, melasma and PIH Superficial acne scars Fine lines and photodamage Body areas: back acne, elbow and knee darkening, underarm pigmentation, rough heels Active herpes simplex anywhere on the face or treatment area Active bacterial or fungal skin infection in or near the area Open wounds, cuts, abrasions or erosions in the area Severe active acne with large pustules or cysts Active eczema, psoriasis or dermatitis flare in the area Sunburned or recently tanned skin (last 2 weeks) Vitiligo in the treatment area Pregnancy — especially Yellow Peel and Phenol (category X) Breastfeeding Active cancer or on chemotherapy / radiotherapy Uncontrolled diabetes Autoimmune skin conditions: Lupus, Dermatomyositis, Pemphigus Porphyria or photosensitivity disorder Cardiac conditions (Phenol only) Liver or kidney disease (Phenol only) Isotretinoin within the last 6 months Medium or deep peel in the same area within 4 weeks Laser or IPL in the same area within 4 weeks Microneedling in the same area within 2 weeks Waxing, threading or depilation in the area within 48 hours Sunbed use within 2 weeks Photosensitising antibiotics: tetracycline, doxycycline, ciprofloxacin Topical or oral retinoids in active use High-dose systemic steroids Aspirin / salicylate allergy — for Salicylic and Jessner's peels Fitzpatrick V–VI for any peel above superficial — high PIH risk History of PIH — patch test mandatory History of keloid or hypertrophic scarring Thyroid disorders — Jessner's (resorcinol component) PCOS or hormonal melasma — the hormonal driver must be ad"},{"p":"tx-cosmelan","t":"Cosmelan Depigmentation System","g":"tx","s":"Protocol · Skin & Dermatology","k":"Cosmelan Depigmentation System Cosmelan 1 + Cosmelan 2 home phase A professional depigmentation system that suppresses melanin production at the enzyme level, used for stubborn melasma and hormonal hyperpigmentation — including on darker skin types. A combination of azelaic, kojic, phytic and ascorbic acids with retinol inhibits tyrosinase, the enzyme that produces melanin. The clinic mask starts the process; the Cosmelan 2 home cream sustains the suppression. Without the home phase, results are incomplete and pigmentation returns. Melasma Hormonal hyperpigmentation and chloasma Post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation Sun spots and freckles Fitzpatrick III–VI Pregnancy or breastfeeding Active skin infection or open wounds in the area Known allergy to any component Isotretinoin within the last 6 months Active herpes simplex on the face Severe active acne with cysts Very sensitive or reactive skin — patch test first History of severe PIH Client unwilling or unable to commit to the home phase — do not start Concurrent laser or peel course — space appropriately 6–10 hours — client removes at home at the exact prescribed time Cosmelan 2 cream three times daily Cosmelan 2 cream twice daily Cosmelan 2 once daily for maintenance SPF 50+ every morning throughout — non-negotiable Clinic follow-up at Day 30 Assess and prescribe Dermatologist confirms diagnosis, Fitzpatrick type and suitability. Photograph under standard lighting. Sell the full system Confirm the client has the Cosmelan 2 home cream and SPF 50+ before starting. Do not apply the mask without them. Cleanse and degrease Double cleanse, then degrease to remove all surface oil. Apply the mask Even layer over the full treatment area. Avoid eyes, nostrils and lips. Written wash-off time Give the client a written card with the exact clock time to wash the mask off at home — 6 to 10 hours from application. Home removal Client rinses with cool water and gentle cleanser at the exact time, then applies the prescribed post cream. Home phase Days 1–7: Cosmelan 2 three times daily. Days 7–30: twice daily. Month 2 onwards: once daily. SPF 50+ every morning. Day 30 review Clinic assessment and phase 2 guidance. Photograph and c"},{"p":"tx-hydra","t":"HydraFacial & Medi-Facials","g":"tx","s":"Protocol · Skin & Dermatology","k":"HydraFacial & Medi-Facials HydraFacial Deep Cleanse · Hydration Therapy · Insta Glow · Skin Hydration Face A multi-step device facial that cleanses, exfoliates, extracts and infuses serums in one session, with no downtime. A vortex tip delivers a cleansing and exfoliating solution while suction lifts debris out of the pores. Acid infusion loosens congestion, extraction clears it, and a final hydrating and antioxidant serum is infused into the freshly cleared skin. Congestion, blackheads and enlarged pores Dull, dehydrated skin Uneven texture Mild acne Pre-event skin preparation Maintenance between clinical treatments Active herpes simplex on the face Active bacterial or fungal skin infection Open wounds, sunburn or acute dermatitis in the area Severe active cystic acne Isotretinoin within the last 6 months Known allergy to any serum component Rosacea with active flushing — gentle settings and serum selection Very sensitive or reactive skin Recent peel, laser or microneedling within 2 weeks Pregnancy — avoid acid-heavy serum boosters Adjust to skin sensitivity; lower on thin, reactive or rosacea-prone skin Selected by skin type; omit or reduce for sensitive skin and pregnancy Vortex extraction only. Manual extraction of cysts is not permitted at the therapist level Antioxidant / hydrating serum, then SPF 50+ Consult and consent Confirm indication, skin type, allergies and signed consent. Double cleanse Remove all makeup and SPF. Pat dry. Exfoliation pass Vortex cleanse and exfoliate with the selected tip and solution. Acid infusion Apply the selected booster at the appropriate strength for the skin type. Monitor for tingling and stop early if the client reports burning. Extraction Vortex extraction over congested zones. Do not force closed comedones or cystic lesions. Infusion Hydrating and antioxidant serum infusion. Optional LED / mask LED panel or sheet mask as prescribed. Finish Moisturiser and SPF 50+ before the client leaves. Documentation Log tips, boosters, extraction zones and skin response in ERP. Use a gentle cleanser for 24 hours SPF 50+ daily Drink plenty of water No active skincare for 24–48 hours No steam, sauna or heavy sweating for 24 hours No ma"},{"p":"tx-mnrf","t":"MNRF — Microneedling Radiofrequency","g":"tx","s":"Protocol · Skin & Dermatology","k":"MNRF — Microneedling Radiofrequency Fractional RF · Micro Needle RF · D-MNRF · Morpheus-type RF microneedling Insulated micro-needles deliver radiofrequency energy directly into the dermis, remodelling acne scars, tightening skin and refining pores with minimal surface damage. The needles bypass the epidermis and deposit RF energy at a controlled dermal depth. The thermal injury zones trigger a wound-healing cascade — new collagen and elastin — while the surface heals within days. Because the epidermis is largely spared, PIH risk is lower than with ablative resurfacing, which makes it well suited to Indian skin. Atrophic acne scars (rolling and boxcar) Enlarged pores Skin laxity — face, jawline, neck Fine lines and early ageing Stretch marks (body) Axillary hyperhidrosis [dermatologist protocol] Active herpes simplex in or near the treatment area Active bacterial or fungal skin infection Active inflammatory acne with pustules or cysts in the area Open wounds or dermatitis in the area Pregnancy or breastfeeding Pacemaker or implanted electronic device Metal implants or plates in the treatment area Active cancer or on chemotherapy / radiotherapy Isotretinoin within the last 6 months Keloid formation tendency Uncontrolled diabetes Bleeding disorders or active anticoagulation Under 18 years History of PIH — conservative depth and energy, patch test History of keloid Recent filler in the area — space by 2 weeks minimum Recent peel or laser within 4 weeks Immunosuppression Herpes history — prophylactic antiviral required 0.5–2.0 mm by zone [confirm against machine IFU] 0.5–1.0 mm, conservative Set by dermatologist per skin type and indication 1–2 passes; do not stack over the same coordinate at the same depth Topical anaesthetic 30–45 minutes, removed completely before treatment Antiviral cover for any client with herpes history Pre-treatment checklist Complete FORM 04 Section A. Confirm consent, screen contraindications, confirm no isotretinoin, photograph the area. Antiviral cover Confirm prophylactic antiviral was started where a herpes history exists. Cleanse and numb Double cleanse. Apply topical anaesthetic for 30–45 minutes under occlusion. Remove numbing and d"},{"p":"tx-dermapen","t":"Derma Pen / Microneedling","g":"tx","s":"Protocol · Skin & Dermatology","k":"Derma Pen / Microneedling Dermapen · Dermaroll · Collagen Induction Therapy Controlled micro-injury with fine needles that triggers the skin's own collagen production, used for scars, texture, pores and overall rejuvenation. Thousands of micro-channels create a controlled wound-healing response — inflammation, proliferation and remodelling — producing new collagen and elastin. The channels also allow serums to penetrate far more effectively than topical application alone. Acne scars and textural irregularity Enlarged pores Fine lines Stretch marks Dull skin and uneven tone Serum and growth-factor delivery Active herpes simplex in or near the area Active bacterial or fungal infection Active inflammatory or cystic acne in the area Open wounds or dermatitis in the area Pregnancy or breastfeeding Isotretinoin within the last 6 months Keloid tendency Active cancer or on chemotherapy Bleeding disorder or active anticoagulation Uncontrolled diabetes History of PIH — conservative depth Recent filler in the area within 2 weeks Recent peel or laser within 4 weeks Herpes history — antiviral prophylaxis Immunosuppression 0.25–1.5 mm by zone 1.5–2.0 mm, doctor only 2–4 passes in a cross-hatch pattern to the clinical endpoint Uniform erythema with pinpoint bleeding Single use per client. Never reused Sterile, injectable-grade or purpose-formulated only. Never a cosmetic serum containing Vitamin C or actives during the pass Consult and consent Confirm indication, screen contraindications, signed consent, before photographs. Cleanse and numb Double cleanse. Topical anaesthetic 30–45 minutes. Remove and prep Remove all anaesthetic. Degrease. Antiseptic prep. Fit new cartridge New sterile cartridge for this client only. Set depth Depth per zone — shallowest on forehead, around eyes and neck; deeper on cheeks and scars. Delivery Cross-hatch passes — horizontal, vertical, diagonal — to the endpoint of uniform erythema with pinpoint bleeding. Serum application Sterile serum only during and after the pass. Cooling and barrier Cool compress, sterile saline, barrier cream. Disposal and documentation Cartridge to sharps. Log depth, passes, zones, serum used and response in ERP. Use only"},{"p":"tx-hifuface","t":"HIFU Face & Neck","g":"tx","s":"Protocol · Skin & Dermatology","k":"HIFU Face & Neck HIFU Tightening · SMAS Lift · Non-Surgical Facelift Focused ultrasound delivered at three fixed depths to lift and tighten the face and neck, including the SMAS layer that a surgical facelift targets — without any incision. Energy is focused at 1.5 mm (superficial dermis), 3.0 mm (deep dermis) and 4.5 mm (SMAS / fascial layer), creating discrete coagulation points at depth while the skin surface stays intact. The tissue contracts immediately and then remodels with new collagen over the following 8–12 weeks. Sagging jowls and loss of jawline definition Mid-face and cheek laxity Brow position Neck laxity and early 'turkey neck' Peri-oral and nasolabial softening Overall facial tightening in the 35–60 age group Pregnancy or breastfeeding Cardiac pacemaker or implanted electronic device Metal implants or plates in the treatment field Active skin infection or open wounds in the area Active cystic acne in the field Active cancer or on chemotherapy / radiotherapy Severe or cystic acne in the treatment field Recent surgery in the field within 3 months Uncontrolled systemic disease Dermal fillers in the field — space by a minimum of 2 weeks; discuss possible filler degradation Botox in the field within 2 weeks Very thin or gaunt facial fat pads — risk of a hollowed look Keloid tendency Autoimmune connective tissue disease Bell's palsy history 4.5 mm (SMAS) · 3.0 mm (deep dermis) · 1.5 mm (superficial dermis) Face cartridges on body; body cartridges on face Thyroid gland, major vessels of the neck, the bony orbit, and the marginal mandibular nerve path Logged per cartridge per client in ERP before and after each session Marked on the skin before starting; systematic linear delivery Consult and consent Assess laxity, photograph from standard angles, confirm signed consent and screen contraindications. Cleanse and mark Double cleanse. Mark the treatment grid and clearly mark exclusion zones — thyroid, orbital rim, major neck vessels. Numbing Topical anaesthetic where required, removed completely before starting. Shot count check Check and log the remaining shot count on each cartridge before beginning. Gel Standard ultrasound gel, full even coverage. Depth "},{"p":"tx-rfface","t":"RF Face Tightening & Contouring","g":"tx","s":"Protocol · Skin & Dermatology","k":"RF Face Tightening & Contouring RF Face & Chin · V-Face · RF Anti-Ageing Gentle radiofrequency heating of the facial dermis to tighten skin, define the jawline and soften jowls, with no downtime. Controlled RF heat contracts existing collagen immediately and stimulates fibroblasts to lay down new collagen over the following weeks. Facial settings are much lower than body settings because facial skin is thinner and closer to bone. Early jowling and jawline softening Mild facial laxity Double chin contouring (with 1 MHz cavitation head, never facial cavitation) Post-weight-loss facial laxity Maintenance between HIFU sessions NEVER use ultrasound cavitation on the face — RF only. No exceptions Pregnancy or lactation Cardiac pacemaker or implanted electronic device Metal implants or plates in the facial area Active skin infection or open wounds in the area Active cancer or malignancy Recent facial surgery within 3 months Thyroid disorder — do not treat the front of the neck without clearance Recent filler or Botox in the area within 2 weeks Rosacea with active flushing Very thin or fragile skin Peripheral neuropathy 10–15 W ONLY 40–42°C 45°C Slow circular strokes, constant movement. Never static Thyroid area at the front of the neck; the bony orbit Screen and consent Screen contraindications, confirm signed consent, photograph. Cleanse Remove all makeup and SPF. Pat dry. Apply coupling gel RF conductive gel or coupling cream, even coverage. Set parameters 10–15 W for face and jowls. Start at the lower end. Delivery Slow circular strokes, constant movement. Work zone by zone. Temperature monitoring Target 40–42°C. Check regularly. STOP at 45°C. Remove gel and finish Remove gel, apply soothing serum and SPF 50+. Documentation Zone, power, duration, response in ERP. SPF 50+ daily Drink water Expect mild flushing for an hour No hot bath, sauna or steam for 24 hours No actives for 24 hours Mild flushing Thermal burn Prolonged erythema Any cavitation attempted on the face Therapist without facial RF clearance operating Temperature above 44°C Treatment over the thyroid RF facial handpiece · V-Face Machine LIFE-SOP-NSLS-001 §3.4 (facial RF rule) · Skin Room SOP"},{"p":"tx-skinprp","t":"Skin PRP & Vampire Facial","g":"tx","s":"Protocol · Skin & Dermatology","k":"Skin PRP & Vampire Facial Platelet Rich Plasma — Face · PRP Skin Rejuvenation The client's own concentrated platelets, drawn from their blood and reintroduced into the skin, to accelerate repair, improve texture and stimulate collagen. Blood is drawn and spun in a centrifuge to separate platelet-rich plasma. Platelets carry growth factors — PDGF, TGF-β, VEGF — that drive fibroblast activity, angiogenesis and collagen synthesis. The PRP is delivered by injection or through microneedling channels. Dull, tired skin and loss of glow Fine lines and early ageing Acne scars (with microneedling) Under-eye hollowing and dark circles Post-procedure healing acceleration Platelet disorders, thrombocytopenia or bleeding disorder Active anticoagulation therapy Active infection at the treatment or draw site Sepsis or systemic infection Active cancer, especially haematological malignancy Pregnancy or breastfeeding Severe anaemia Chronic liver disease Active herpes in the treatment field Recent NSAID use — reduces platelet function; advise a 7-day gap Smoking — reduces efficacy Poorly controlled diabetes Autoimmune connective tissue disease Isotretinoin within 6 months Per kit protocol Speed and time strictly per the kit IFU. Calibrate weekly PRP must be used promptly after spinning per kit protocol Tube labelled with client name and ID at the chair, in front of the client — never batch-labelled Injection and/or microneedling channels Consult and consent Confirm indication and screen contraindications. Signed FORM 20. Photograph. Blood draw Aseptic draw into the labelled vacutainer tubes. Label at the chair in front of the client. Centrifuge Spin strictly per kit protocol. Confirm clean separation. Prepare the field Cleanse, numb if required, remove anaesthetic, antiseptic prep. Extract PRP Draw off the platelet-rich layer using aseptic technique. Delivery Inject per the mapped plan and/or deliver through microneedling channels. Post-treatment Cool compress. Sterile barrier. No makeup for 12–24 hours. Disposal All sharps and tubes directly to biohazard sharps. Never reuse. Documentation Complete FORM 21. Log volume, sites, technique and response in ERP. Expect redness and mild s"},{"p":"tx-ivdrip","t":"IV Skin Glow & Antioxidant Drips","g":"tx","s":"Protocol · Skin & Dermatology","k":"IV Skin Glow & Antioxidant Drips Glutathione IV · Vitamin C Infusion · Gluta Glow · IV Anti-Ageing Drip An intravenous infusion of antioxidants — most commonly glutathione with Vitamin C — used for skin brightening, antioxidant support and general wellness. Glutathione is the body's principal intracellular antioxidant and inhibits tyrosinase, shifting melanin production toward lighter pheomelanin. Vitamin C regenerates glutathione and supports collagen synthesis. IV delivery bypasses gut absorption limits. Overall skin brightening and evenness Antioxidant and wellness support Adjunct to a pigmentation programme Post-sun-damage support Pregnancy or breastfeeding Known allergy to glutathione, Vitamin C or any infusion component G6PD deficiency (high-dose Vitamin C — haemolysis risk) Renal impairment or history of oxalate kidney stones (high-dose Vitamin C) Active asthma with sulphite sensitivity Active cancer without oncologist clearance Severe cardiac or renal failure — fluid load Anaemia History of drug allergy or atopy — slower infusion rate, closer monitoring Diabetes — glucose interaction with high-dose Vitamin C Poor venous access Slow. Never rushed — most reactions are rate-related Doctor on site for the entire infusion — mandatory Adrenaline, antihistamine, hydrocortisone and oxygen available in the room before the line is started BP and pulse before, during and after Per product IFU; cold chain where required, with a daily temperature log Doctor pre-drip assessment History, allergies, G6PD status where indicated, renal history. Record vitals. Document the assessment. Consent Signed consent, including the off-label nature of skin-lightening use and known risks. Emergency readiness Confirm the emergency kit and oxygen are in the room and in date before starting. Prepare the infusion Aseptic preparation. Check product, batch, expiry and dose against the prescription. Second-person check. Cannulation Aseptic IV access. Secure the line. Test period Start slowly and observe closely for the first 5 minutes for any reaction. Infusion Run at the prescribed rate. Check the client every 10 minutes. Record vitals. Completion Flush, remove the cannula, apply pressure "},{"p":"tx-botox","t":"Botulinum Toxin (Botox)","g":"tx","s":"Protocol · Skin & Dermatology","k":"Botulinum Toxin (Botox) Botox · Anti-wrinkle Injection · Botulinum Toxin Type A A prescription injectable that temporarily relaxes specific facial muscles to soften dynamic wrinkles, slim the jaw, or reduce sweating. Botulinum toxin type A blocks acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction, temporarily reducing muscle contraction. The overlying skin stops creasing and existing lines soften. The effect wears off as new nerve terminals form over 3–4 months. Glabellar frown lines Forehead lines Crow's feet Bunny lines and gummy smile Masseter reduction / jaw slimming Platysmal neck bands Axillary hyperhidrosis (excess sweating) Pregnancy or breastfeeding Neuromuscular disorders: myasthenia gravis, Lambert-Eaton syndrome, ALS Known hypersensitivity to botulinum toxin or albumin Active infection at the injection site Aminoglycoside antibiotics in current use — potentiates the toxin Body dysmorphic presentation or unrealistic fixed expectations Bleeding disorder or anticoagulation — bruising risk Previous unsatisfactory result or ptosis with toxin Asymmetry present before treatment — must be photographed and discussed Recent facial surgery Per product IFU with preservative-free saline. Record reconstitution time 2–8°C cold chain. Daily temperature log Recorded per injection point on the pictorial record sheet — FORM 13 3 months to the same area to reduce antibody formation Client stays upright for 4 hours and does not rub the area Consult and assess Assess muscle action dynamically. Photograph at rest and in animation. Document pre-existing asymmetry. Consent Signed consent covering onset time, duration, bruising, ptosis risk and the need for possible touch-up. Reconstitute Per IFU. Record product, batch, expiry and reconstitution time. Mark injection points Mark points with the client animating. Photograph the marked plan. Antisepsis Clean the field with an antiseptic. Allow to dry. Inject Deliver the planned units per point. Record every point and unit on FORM 13. Immediate post Do not massage. Cold compress only if bruising. Client remains upright. Sharps and documentation Sharps to biohazard. Log product, batch, total units, points and response in ERP and "},{"p":"tx-filler","t":"Dermal Fillers","g":"tx","s":"Protocol · Skin & Dermatology","k":"Dermal Fillers Hyaluronic Acid Filler · Juvederm · Volbella · Voluma · Volux · Volite Injectable hyaluronic acid gel used to restore volume, define contours and smooth static lines. Cross-linked hyaluronic acid gel is placed in a specific tissue plane to add volume and structure. It also draws water, adding hydration. It is gradually broken down by the body over 6–18 months, and can be dissolved with hyaluronidase if needed. Cheek and mid-face volume loss Nasolabial folds and marionette lines Lip volume and definition Chin and jawline definition Tear trough hollowing Hand rejuvenation Skin quality boosters (Profhilo, Skinvive, Volite, Viscoderm) Pregnancy or breastfeeding Active skin infection or inflammation at the injection site Known hypersensitivity to hyaluronic acid or lidocaine Active autoimmune disease flare Severe allergy history or anaphylaxis history Bleeding disorder or active anticoagulation without physician clearance Permanent or semi-permanent filler previously placed in the same area Body dysmorphic presentation Recent dental work — space by 2 weeks (bacteraemia risk) Herpes history around the lips — antiviral prophylaxis required for lip filler Prior filler of unknown type in the area Planned surgery or vaccination within 2 weeks History of keloid Must be in stock, in date and immediately accessible before ANY filler is injected — non-negotiable Aspirate before injecting in high-risk zones. Slow, low-pressure delivery. Small aliquots Glabella, nasal dorsum, nasolabial fold, tear trough, temple — highest vascular occlusion risk Product, batch, expiry, volume and plane recorded per area Prophylaxis for any client with a herpes history receiving lip filler Consult and assess Assess anatomy, discuss the plan and limitations, photograph from standard angles. Consent Signed FORM 18. Explicitly cover vascular occlusion, blindness risk in high-risk zones, bruising, swelling, asymmetry, nodules and the possibility of dissolving. Confirm hyaluronidase Confirm hyaluronidase is in stock, in date and immediately to hand. Do NOT proceed without it. Antisepsis Remove all makeup. Antiseptic prep of the field. Sterile technique throughout. Anaesthesia Topical a"},{"p":"tx-boosters","t":"Skin Boosters & Bio-remodelling","g":"tx","s":"Protocol · Skin & Dermatology","k":"Skin Boosters & Bio-remodelling Profhilo · Skinvive · Volite · Viscoderm · Mesotherapy Skin Injectable hyaluronic acid that hydrates and bio-remodels the skin rather than adding volume — improving glow, firmness and fine crepiness. Highly concentrated, minimally cross-linked hyaluronic acid spreads through the tissue plane rather than staying as a bolus. It hydrates and stimulates four types of collagen and elastin, improving skin quality without changing facial shape. Skin laxity and crepiness Dull, dehydrated skin Fine lines without volume loss Neck, décolletage and hands Clients who want improvement without looking 'filled' Pregnancy or breastfeeding Active skin infection or inflammation at the injection site Known hypersensitivity to hyaluronic acid Active autoimmune disease flare Bleeding disorder or active anticoagulation without clearance Recent dental work within 2 weeks Herpes history in the area — antiviral cover Planned surgery or vaccination within 2 weeks Keloid history Defined anatomical points per product protocol — typically 5 per side for facial Profhilo Subdermal / deep dermal per product IFU In stock and in date before injecting At 4 weeks — the protocol is not complete after one session Consult and consent Assess skin quality, photograph, signed consent. Confirm hyaluronidase In stock, in date and immediately accessible. Antisepsis Remove makeup. Antiseptic prep. Mark the points Mark the defined anatomical injection points per protocol. Inject Slow bolus at each point in the correct plane. Aspirate where the protocol requires. Immediate post Expect visible blebs that settle within hours. Do not massage unless the product protocol requires it. Documentation Product, batch, expiry, volume, points in ERP and the Injectable Register. Book the second session at 4 weeks. Expect small raised bumps that settle within 4–24 hours Cold compress if bruised Attend the second session at 4 weeks — the protocol needs both Report increasing pain or colour change immediately No massage unless told otherwise No sauna, steam or intense exercise for 48 hours No dental work for 2 weeks No facial or energy device on the area for 2 weeks Blebs and mild bruising Pers"},{"p":"tx-threads","t":"Thread Lift","g":"tx","s":"Protocol · Skin & Dermatology","k":"Thread Lift PDO Threads · COG Threads · Mono Threads · Non-Surgical Lift Absorbable barbed or smooth sutures placed under the skin to lift sagging tissue and stimulate collagen along the thread path. COG (barbed) threads anchor into the tissue and mechanically reposition it upward. Mono (smooth) threads do not lift but stimulate collagen along their length. Both are made of polydioxanone, which dissolves over 6–8 months while the collagen it induced persists. Mid-face and jowl sagging Jawline definition Brow lift Neck laxity Collagen stimulation (mono threads) Pregnancy or breastfeeding Active skin infection or inflammation in the field Known allergy to polydioxanone Autoimmune disease flare Bleeding disorder or active anticoagulation Active cancer or on chemotherapy Severe skin laxity where the result would be inadequate — refer for surgical opinion Very thin skin — thread visibility risk Previous thread lift with residual material Keloid tendency Recent filler in the same plane Smoking — impairs healing COG for lifting; mono for collagen stimulation Marked and photographed before insertion Facial nerve branches, parotid duct, major vessels — anatomical knowledge is mandatory Full sterile field, sterile drape, sterile gloves. This is a minor surgical procedure Per doctor protocol Consult and assess Assess laxity and skin thickness. Photograph from standard angles including animation. Discuss realistic lift. Consent Signed FORM 15 covering visible threads, extrusion, dimpling, asymmetry, infection and limited longevity. Mark the plan Mark entry points and vectors with the client upright. Photograph the marked plan. Sterile preparation Full antisepsis, sterile drape, sterile gloves and instruments. Local anaesthesia Entry point anaesthesia and along the vector as required. Thread placement Insert in the correct plane along the marked vector. Engage and lift. Check symmetry with the client sitting up. Trim and close Trim thread ends below the skin surface. Confirm no thread is palpable at the surface. Immediate post Cold compress. Antibiotic cover per protocol. Detailed movement restrictions given in writing. Documentation Thread type, number, batch, vectors, entr"},{"p":"tx-lesion","t":"Mole, Wart, Skin Tag & Keloid Management","g":"tx","s":"Protocol · Skin & Dermatology","k":"Mole, Wart, Skin Tag & Keloid Management Naevus Removal · Wart Cautery · Skin Tag Removal · Kenacort for Keloid Removal or treatment of benign skin lesions — moles, warts, skin tags, milia — and intralesional steroid treatment for keloid and hypertrophic scars. Radiofrequency cautery or cryotherapy destroys the lesion tissue at a controlled depth, leaving a small wound that heals by secondary intention. Keloids are treated with intralesional triamcinolone, which flattens the scar by suppressing fibroblast activity. Benign moles and naevi (cosmetic removal) Viral warts and verrucae Skin tags (acrochordons) Milia and syringoma Keloid and hypertrophic scars (intralesional steroid) Seborrhoeic keratoses ANY lesion with suspicious features — asymmetry, irregular border, colour variation, diameter over 6 mm, recent change, bleeding or itching. These must be referred for dermatology assessment and histopathology, NEVER cosmetically destroyed Active infection at the site Pregnancy — elective removal deferred Bleeding disorder or active anticoagulation without clearance Uncontrolled diabetes — poor healing Keloid tendency for elective cosmetic removal Keloid-prone site: chest, shoulders, upper back, jawline Fitzpatrick V–VI — higher PIH and keloid risk; counsel carefully Immunosuppression — recurrent warts Multiple lesions — stage across sessions Mandatory doctor assessment of every pigmented lesion before removal. If in doubt — biopsy, do not destroy Any lesion sent for histology must be recorded with the lab, date and report tracked to closure Controlled, superficial. Over-treatment causes scarring Intralesional triamcinolone, dose per lesion size, repeat at 3–4 week intervals Doctor assessment Full lesion assessment including dermoscopy where available. Screen for malignancy features. Photograph with a scale marker. Decision Suspicious features = refer / biopsy. Never cosmetically destroy a lesion you have not confidently assessed as benign. Consent Signed consent covering scarring, pigmentation change, recurrence and — for pigmented lesions — the loss of histology if destroyed. Antisepsis and anaesthesia Antiseptic prep. Local anaesthetic infiltration. Removal RF cau"},{"p":"tx-pmu","t":"Permanent Makeup (PMU) & Microblading","g":"tx","s":"Protocol · Skin & Dermatology","k":"Permanent Makeup (PMU) & Microblading Microblading Eyebrows · Lip Micropigmentation · Cosmetic Tattooing Cosmetic pigment implanted into the upper dermis to shape eyebrows, define lips or enhance the lash line, lasting 12–24 months. Pigment is deposited into the superficial dermis with a blade or machine needle. Because it sits shallower than a body tattoo, the body gradually breaks it down, so the result fades over 12–24 months rather than being permanent in the tattoo sense. Sparse, thin or asymmetric eyebrows Alopecia and post-chemotherapy brow loss Lip colour correction and definition Lip pigmentation camouflage Scar camouflage in the brow area Pregnancy or breastfeeding Active skin infection, acne or dermatitis in the area Active herpes simplex — for lip PMU, antiviral prophylaxis is mandatory and active outbreak is an absolute bar Keloid tendency Bleeding disorder or active anticoagulation Isotretinoin within the last 6 months Active cancer or on chemotherapy / radiotherapy Uncontrolled diabetes Known allergy to pigment components — patch test mandatory Under 18 years Very oily skin — pigment retention is poorer, counsel expectations Previous PMU with unknown pigment — colour correction is complex Recent botox or filler in the area — space by 2 weeks Keloid-prone skin Immunosuppression Superficial dermis only. Too deep = blue-grey migration and permanence Single use, sterile, opened in front of the client. Never reused Batch and expiry recorded. Patch test 48 hours before first session Mandatory prophylaxis for lip PMU with any herpes history At 4–6 weeks — the result is not complete without it Consult and patch test Discuss shape, colour and expectations. Patch test 48 hours before. Photograph. Consent Signed consent covering fading, colour shift, need for touch-up, infection and the semi-permanent nature. Antiviral cover For lip PMU with any herpes history — antiviral started per doctor prescription before the session. Design and approve Draw the shape with the client sitting upright. Client approves in the mirror and signs off on the design before any pigment. Numbing Topical anaesthetic. Remove completely before starting. Sterile setup Open new sterile"},{"p":"tx-lhr","t":"Laser Hair Removal (Diode)","g":"tx","s":"Protocol · Laser Hair Removal","k":"Laser Hair Removal (Diode) LHR · Laser Hair Reduction · Diode Laser Targeted laser energy that disables hair follicles during their active growth phase, permanently reducing unwanted hair over a course of sessions. Selective photothermolysis. The 808 nm diode wavelength is absorbed by melanin in the hair shaft and follicle. The heat generated destroys the follicle's ability to produce new hair while contact cooling protects the epidermis. Only hairs in the active (anagen) phase respond, which is why multiple sessions spaced across the growth cycle are required. Face — upper lip, chin, sideburns, beard line, full face Underarms Arms — half and full Legs — half and full Back and chest Bikini and Brazilian Full body Male grooming — beard shaping, back and chest Active skin infection, open wounds or active herpes in the treatment area Pregnancy (any trimester) Known photosensitivity disorder or light-triggered seizures Active skin cancer or history of melanoma Isotretinoin (Accutane) within the last 6 months Tattoos or permanent makeup in the direct treatment area Tanned skin — natural or artificial — in the last 4 weeks Fitzpatrick Type V or VI — dermatologist review mandatory Anticoagulant, photosensitising or hormonal medications History of keloid or hypertrophic scarring Active autoimmune disorders — lupus, vitiligo, psoriasis in the area Pacemaker or metal implants near the treatment area History of gold therapy Poorly controlled diabetes PCOS — hormonal regrowth; counsel that maintenance sessions will be needed 808 nm core · 755 nm / 940 nm on multi-wavelength models 5–120 J/cm², set by skin type and body area 5 ms – 400 ms; longer for darker skin types 1–10 Hz 12×12 mm to 12×24 mm by handpiece Integrated contact cooling, 0°C to 5°C tip Sliding or stamping, 10–20% overlap, full coverage Minimum 10 minutes before treatment Client verification Confirm client name, ID, session number and treatment area against ERP and the treatment file. Consent check Confirm the signed consent form is in the file for this session. Skin assessment Re-examine the area. Check for active lesions, cuts, rash or tan. If found, do not proceed. Area preparation Cleanse with a gentle non"},{"p":"tx-hairprp","t":"Hair PRP","g":"tx","s":"Protocol · Hair Treatment","k":"Hair PRP Platelet Rich Plasma — Scalp · Booster PRP · Hair PRP Basic The client's own concentrated platelets injected into the scalp to reduce hair fall and stimulate weakened follicles into stronger growth. Platelets release growth factors — PDGF, VEGF, IGF-1 — that increase blood supply to the dermal papilla, prolong the anagen (growth) phase and thicken miniaturised follicles. It strengthens existing hair; it cannot regrow follicles that are completely lost. Androgenetic alopecia — male and female pattern Telogen effluvium and diffuse thinning Post-partum hair fall Hair thinning after weight loss or illness Adjunct after hair transplant to support graft survival Platelet disorders, thrombocytopenia or bleeding disorder Active anticoagulation therapy Active scalp infection — folliculitis, fungal infection, open wounds Sepsis or systemic infection Active cancer, especially haematological malignancy Pregnancy or breastfeeding Severe anaemia Chronic liver disease Uncontrolled diabetes Recent NSAID use — reduces platelet function; advise a 7-day gap Smoking — measurably reduces efficacy Active seborrhoeic dermatitis — treat the scalp first Untreated thyroid disorder or iron deficiency — treat the cause first or results will be poor Isotretinoin within 6 months Per kit protocol Speed and time strictly per kit IFU. Calibrate weekly and log Dermal / subdermal, approximately 4–6 mm 1 cm grid across the thinning zone Tube labelled with client name and ID at the chair, in front of the client Optional topical or ring block; scalp is sensitive Consult and diagnose Confirm the type of hair loss. Trichoscopy where available. Order thyroid, ferritin, Vitamin D and CBC where indicated — treating PRP without correcting a deficiency wastes the course. Consent and photos Signed FORM 20. Standardised scalp photographs — same parting, same lighting, same angles. Blood draw Aseptic draw into labelled vacutainer tubes. Label at the chair in front of the client. Centrifuge Spin per kit protocol. Confirm clean separation of the platelet layer. Prepare the scalp Part and clean the scalp with antiseptic. Numb if required. Extract PRP Draw off the platelet-rich layer aseptically. Injecti"},{"p":"tx-gfc","t":"GFC — Growth Factor Concentrate","g":"tx","s":"Protocol · Hair Treatment","k":"GFC — Growth Factor Concentrate Advanced GFC · Growth Factor Therapy · GFC Scalp A refinement of PRP in which the platelets are deliberately activated before injection, releasing a higher and more consistent concentration of pure growth factors. Blood is drawn and processed with a kit that activates the platelets, releasing their growth factor payload into the plasma. The resulting concentrate is acellular and higher in growth factor content than standard PRP, which is why fewer sessions are typically needed. Androgenetic alopecia Diffuse thinning and telogen effluvium Clients who have plateaued on standard PRP Post-transplant graft support Platelet disorders, thrombocytopenia or bleeding disorder Active anticoagulation therapy Active scalp infection Active cancer, especially haematological malignancy Pregnancy or breastfeeding Severe anaemia Chronic liver disease Sepsis or systemic infection Recent NSAID use — 7-day gap advised Smoking Untreated thyroid disorder or iron deficiency — correct first Active seborrhoeic dermatitis Follow the specific GFC kit IFU exactly — activation timing is critical Use within the kit-specified window after activation Dermal / subdermal, 4–6 mm 1 cm grid At the chair, in front of the client Consult and diagnose Confirm the hair loss type. Order and review baseline bloods where indicated. Consent and photos Signed consent. Standardised scalp photographs. Blood draw Aseptic draw into labelled kit tubes. Process and activate Follow the kit IFU exactly — spin, activation and second spin timings are not interchangeable between kits. Prepare the scalp Part and clean with antiseptic. Numb if required. Injection 1 cm grid at 4–6 mm depth across the thinning zone. Aftercare 24-hour no-wash rule. Written aftercare. Disposal and documentation Sharps to biohazard. Log kit brand, batch, volume, zones and response in ERP. Book the next session. Same aftercare as PRP — no wash for 24 hours Take prescribed supplements Complete the full course No wash for 24 hours No chemical hair treatment for 72 hours No NSAIDs for 48 hours Tenderness and swelling Headache Infection Kit activation timing not followed Active scalp infection Client on anticoagulan"},{"p":"tx-scalp","t":"Scalp Treatments — Anti-Hairfall, Anti-Dandruff & HFT","g":"tx","s":"Protocol · Hair Treatment","k":"Scalp Treatments — Anti-Hairfall, Anti-Dandruff & HFT HFT High Frequency Therapy · Scalp Peel · Anti-Dandruff Treatment · Anti-Hairfall Therapy · Mesotherapy Hair A group of scalp-directed treatments — exfoliating peels, high-frequency therapy, low-level laser and nutrient mesotherapy — used to treat the scalp environment so hair can grow properly. Scalp peels clear follicular build-up and flaking. High-frequency current is antimicrobial and increases local circulation. Low-level laser stimulates cellular metabolism in the follicle. Mesotherapy delivers vitamins, peptides and vasodilators directly to the dermal papilla. Most hair loss cases need the scalp treated before follicle-stimulating treatments will work. Dandruff and seborrhoeic dermatitis Oily, congested scalp Diffuse hair fall Itchy or flaky scalp Preparation of the scalp before PRP or GFC Post-transplant scalp care Open wounds, weeping lesions or active bacterial infection on the scalp Active fungal infection requiring systemic treatment — treat first Pregnancy — for mesotherapy injections Pacemaker — for high-frequency therapy Epilepsy — for high-frequency and photo therapy Known allergy to any mesotherapy component Bleeding disorder — for mesotherapy Recent hair transplant — timing per the surgeon Very sensitive or reactive scalp Psoriasis flare on the scalp — dermatologist direction Active anticoagulation — for mesotherapy Per product; typically 5–15 minutes Indirect or direct method per protocol; never over broken skin Superficial dermal, 2–4 mm Per device IFU; eye protection required Peel → treatment serum or mesotherapy → high frequency or laser → leave-on Consult and assess Trichoscopy where available. Assess scalp condition, hair density and pull test. Review investigations. Consent Signed consent, particularly for any injectable component. Section the scalp Part into clean sections for even product application. Scalp peel Apply the prescribed peel to the scalp, not the hair. Observe the contact time. Rinse thoroughly. Treatment serum or mesotherapy Apply the prescribed serum, or — doctor only — inject the mesotherapy cocktail at 2–4 mm in a 1 cm grid. High frequency or laser Apply per protoco"},{"p":"tx-ht","t":"Hair Transplant (FUE)","g":"tx","s":"Protocol · Hair Transplant & Restoration","k":"Hair Transplant (FUE) Follicular Unit Extraction · HT · Hair Restoration Surgery A surgical procedure in which individual follicular units are extracted from the permanent donor zone at the back and sides of the scalp and implanted into thinning or bald areas. Follicles in the occipital donor zone are genetically resistant to DHT, the hormone that miniaturises hair in pattern baldness. When transplanted, they retain that resistance and continue to grow in their new site for life. The transplanted hair sheds at 2–4 weeks — this is normal — and regrows from the retained follicle at 3–4 months. Male pattern baldness — Norwood grades II–VI with an adequate donor Female pattern hair loss with a stable donor zone Frontal hairline restoration Crown filling Beard and moustache transplant Eyebrow transplant Scar camouflage — post-surgical or burn scars Insufficient donor density for meaningful coverage Active scalp infection or inflammatory scalp disease Uncontrolled diabetes or uncontrolled hypertension Bleeding disorder or active anticoagulation not cleared by a physician Active cancer or on chemotherapy Unstable cardiac disease Keloid tendency on the scalp Body dysmorphic presentation or fixed unrealistic expectations Alopecia areata in an active phase Under 18 years Age under 25 — counsel on future progression and the need for medical therapy Smoking — significantly impairs graft survival; advise cessation 2 weeks before and after Diffuse unpatterned alopecia — poor donor quality Untreated thyroid disorder or severe anaemia Isotretinoin within 6 months Client not willing to take medical therapy to protect native hair Density per cm², scalp laxity and Norwood/Ludwig grade documented before booking Planned and agreed in writing at consultation; actual count recorded at the end of surgery Local (tumescent). Full monitoring and emergency kit in theatre Grafts held in the specified holding solution at the specified temperature — minimise time Day 1 wash, Day 7, Day 15, Month 3, Month 6, Month 12 Consultation Surgeon assesses donor density, scalp laxity, Norwood/Ludwig grade and family pattern. Trichoscopy. Photograph from standard angles. Agree the graft plan and hairline"},{"p":"tx-nshr","t":"Non-Surgical Hair Restoration (Hair Patch / System)","g":"tx","s":"Protocol · Hair Transplant & Restoration","k":"Non-Surgical Hair Restoration (Hair Patch / System) NSHR · Hair Patch · Hair System · Non-Surgical Hair Replacement A custom-made hair system fitted to the scalp that provides immediate, complete coverage for clients who are not candidates for transplant or who want an instant result. A base of lace, skin or a hybrid material is templated to the client's scalp and ventilated with human hair matched for colour, texture, density and curl. It is attached with medical adhesive, tape or clips and serviced regularly as the natural scalp oils break down the bond. Advanced baldness with inadequate donor for transplant Clients who want an immediate result Alopecia areata totalis / universalis Post-chemotherapy hair loss Scarring alopecia Clients unwilling to undergo surgery Active scalp infection, dermatitis or open wounds Known allergy to the adhesive or base material — patch test mandatory Active scalp psoriasis or seborrhoeic dermatitis flare — treat first Very oily scalp — shorter bond life, counsel expectations Heavy sweating or contact sport — discuss attachment method Client unwilling to commit to servicing — the system will look poor within weeks Adhesive patch test 48 hours before the first fitting — mandatory Custom scalp template taken and stored with the client record 3–4 weeks 6–12 months depending on base type and care Consult and patch test Assess the scalp, discuss options and expectations, take the adhesive patch test 48 hours before fitting. Photograph. Template and match Take a precise scalp template. Match colour, density, texture, curl and hairline design. Document all specifications. Order and record Order the system with full specifications recorded in ERP against the client. Scalp preparation Clean and degrease the attachment zone. Trim or shave the bonding area as agreed. Fitting Position, mark and cut the base to the template. Attach with the agreed method. Blend and style. Client education Demonstrate home care, washing, sleeping and product use. Give the written care card. Service booking Book the first service at 3–4 weeks and explain the ongoing schedule. Documentation System specification, base type, attachment method, service schedule in E"},{"p":"tx-bca","t":"Body Composition Analysis (BCA / InBody)","g":"tx","s":"Protocol · Diagnostics & Assessment","k":"Body Composition Analysis (BCA / InBody) BCA · InBody Composition Analysis · Bioelectrical Impedance Analysis A body composition measurement that separates weight into fat mass, muscle mass, visceral fat and body water — the baseline for every slimming programme. Bioelectrical impedance sends a low, safe electrical current through the body. Lean tissue conducts easily because of its water content; fat resists. The resistance profile is converted into fat mass, skeletal muscle mass, visceral fat level and total body water. Baseline before any slimming programme 4-weekly progress tracking GLP-1 programme monitoring — protecting lean mass is the whole point Demonstrating fat loss when scale weight has not moved Pregnancy Cardiac pacemaker or implanted electronic device — bioimpedance is contraindicated Significant oedema or fluid retention — results will be distorted Dialysis clients Recent heavy exercise or large meal — reschedule Same time of day, empty bladder, no meal or exercise in the previous 2 hours Weight, BMI, fat mass, skeletal muscle mass, visceral fat level, total body water Abdomen at the umbilicus, waist at the narrowest point, hips, thighs, upper arms — standardised tension tape 4-angle standardised photographs at baseline and every 4 weeks Screen Confirm no pacemaker and not pregnant. Confirm preparation conditions were met. Standardise Remove shoes, socks and heavy jewellery. Same clothing weight standard each time. Measure Run the analysis per the machine protocol with correct hand and foot electrode contact. Circumference Take standardised tape measurements at the same anatomical points. Photograph 4 angles — anterior, posterior, left lateral, right lateral — in the fixed-lighting photo booth. Explain Walk the client through fat mass vs muscle mass. Set the expectation that scale weight alone is a poor measure. Documentation Store the report, measurements and photographs in the ERP client record. Come at the same time of day for every analysis Empty your bladder before the test Avoid heavy meals and exercise for 2 hours before No alcohol in the 24 hours before Do not compare readings taken under different conditions None expected Pacemaker or im"},{"p":"tx-tricho","t":"Trichoscopy & Skin Analysis","g":"tx","s":"Protocol · Diagnostics & Assessment","k":"Trichoscopy & Skin Analysis E-Scope Trichoscopy · Skin Analyzer · Digital Scalp Analysis Magnified digital imaging of the scalp or skin that shows follicle density, miniaturisation, scalp condition, pore size, pigmentation and hydration — turning a subjective consultation into a documented baseline. A high-magnification camera with polarised and UV light modes images the scalp or skin surface and sub-surface. Follicle counts, hair shaft diameter variation, sebum, pigment distribution and hydration are read from the images and compared over time. Baseline before any hair loss programme Distinguishing pattern hair loss from telogen effluvium Scalp condition assessment — dandruff, seborrhoea, folliculitis Skin baseline — pores, pigment, hydration, sebum Documenting progress at 3-month intervals Active weeping or infected lesion — image but do not press the probe onto it Heavy oil or product in the hair on the day — reschedule or wash first for a usable image Per device; use the same magnification for every comparison Fixed anatomical sites — vertex, mid-scalp, frontal, occipital donor — imaged the same way every time Probe tip disinfected between every client Same site, same magnification, same lighting mode Prepare Confirm no oil or product. Disinfect the probe tip. Image the fixed sites Vertex, mid-scalp, frontal and occipital donor for hair. Forehead, cheeks, nose and chin for skin. Read the findings Follicle density, shaft diameter variation, single vs multi-hair units, scalp condition. For skin: pore size, pigment, hydration, sebum. Explain to the client Show the images on screen. Explain what is being seen in plain language, without alarming the client. Document Save all images against the client record with the date and site labels. Set the next review at 3 months. Come with a clean, oil-free scalp for hair analysis Come without makeup for skin analysis Attend the 3-month review so progress can be compared Do not oil the scalp on the day of analysis Do not expect a diagnosis from imaging alone — it supports the doctor's assessment None expected Probe tip not disinfected between clients Therapist giving a diagnosis instead of referring findings to the doctor "},{"p":"tx-nutrition","t":"Clinical Nutrition & LIFE RISE Programme","g":"tx","s":"Protocol · LIFE RISE & Nutrition","k":"Clinical Nutrition & LIFE RISE Programme LIFE RISE · Customised Diet Plan · Dietician Consultation · Online Nutrition Programme A structured, medically supervised nutrition programme that prioritises fat loss while actively protecting lean muscle — delivered in clinic or online, alone or alongside a clinical treatment plan. Total Daily Energy Expenditure is calculated from weight, height, age, gender and activity level. A safe, progressive caloric deficit is set with protein held high to preserve lean mass. Behaviour change — food logging, mindful eating, meal structure — is the part that determines whether the result lasts. Weight loss and fat loss programmes Support alongside GLP-1 therapy — protein intake is mandatory, not optional Post-treatment maintenance Metabolic risk — prediabetes, dyslipidaemia, PCOS Skin and hair conditions with a nutritional driver Weight maintenance after a programme completes Dieticians must NOT prescribe or adjust any medication — doctor only Dieticians must NOT diagnose a medical condition Dieticians must NOT advise on GLP-1 dosing or titration Deficits below 1,200 kcal/day without medical supervision Extended fasting beyond 12 hours without medical supervision Pregnancy or breastfeeding — plan must be written with doctor involvement Type 1 diabetes or insulin-treated Type 2 — doctor-supervised plan only Chronic kidney disease — protein targets must be set by the physician History of disordered eating — psychological support alongside, and no restrictive targets Adolescents — growth-appropriate plans only, with guardian involvement Calculated from weight, height, age, gender and activity level. Documented in ERP Safe and progressive as determined by the doctor or nutritionist. Avoid below 1,200 kcal/day without medical supervision Minimum 1.2 g per kg of reference body weight daily. 60–80 g minimum on GLP-1 therapy Complex, low-glycaemic sources only — fibre-rich vegetables, legumes, oats. Avoid refined sugars Omega-3 (fish oil, flaxseed) and monounsaturated (olive oil, avocado). Minimise saturated and trans fats 3–5 small, balanced meals per day. No extended fasting beyond 12 hours without supervision Weekly review of the client"},{"p":"directory","t":"Consultation Charges (1 Visit)","g":"svc","s":"Service · Administrative Services","k":"Administrative Charges · Consultation & Registration · Not Applicable · Accounts / Front Office · — · 1 sessions · One-Time · Consultation Charges"},{"p":"directory","t":"File Charges (1 Entry)","g":"svc","s":"Service · Administrative Services","k":"Administrative Charges · Consultation & Registration · Not Applicable · Accounts / Front Office · — · 1 sessions · One-Time · File Charges"},{"p":"directory","t":"Registration Fees (1 Entry)","g":"svc","s":"