te · Minimum 12 supervised sessions before independent practice · Focal depth physics: 1.5, 3.0 and 4.5 mm on face; 8.0 and 13.0 mm on body Facial anatomy: SMAS, nerve pathways, danger zones, the thyroid Why face and body cartridges are never interchanged Shot count as a clinical and stock record Recognising nerve involvement during treatment Grid marking and exclusion zone marking Cartridge selection, fitting and shot count logging Flush contact technique and line discipline Depth sequencing — deepest first Recognising and responding to radiating pain or twitching"},{"p":"tr-rf","t":"RF Body and Face Tightening — Therapist Certification","g":"train","s":"Training module","k":"Therapist. Facial RF requires separate clearance. · Minimum 8 supervised sessions; facial RF requires a further 5 · RF heating and collagen contraction The 40–43°C window and the 44°C stop rule Metal, pacemaker and IUD contraindications Why facial RF power is capped at 10–15 W Coupling medium selection and application Continuous circular technique Temperature monitoring every 2 minutes Thermal burn prevention response drill"},{"p":"tr-mnrf","t":"MNRF & Microneedling — Doctor and Assistant Module","g":"train","s":"Training module","k":"Doctor performs; assistant supports · Assistant: 10 supervised sessions · Fractional dermal heating and why the epidermis is spared Depth selection by facial zone Why single-use tips are non-negotiable Herpes prophylaxis and who needs it Recognising tram-track marks and what causes them Numbing application and complete removal Sterile field setup Tip fitting and disposal Post-procedure barrier application FORM 04 completion, both sections"},{"p":"tr-inject","t":"Injectables — Botox, Fillers, Threads (Doctor Module)","g":"train","s":"Training module","k":"Doctor only · Institutional credentialling by the Medical Head · Facial vascular anatomy and the high-risk zones for occlusion Vascular occlusion: recognition, the hyaluronidase flood protocol, escalation Blindness risk and the immediate ophthalmic pathway Botulinum toxin: mechanism, dosing, ptosis avoidance and management Thread anatomy, vectors and nerve avoidance Delayed inflammatory nodules and their triggers Aspiration technique and low-pressure delivery drill Hyaluronidase reconstitution and flood technique — rehearsed, not read Pictorial record completion (FORM 13) Sterile technique for thread placement"},{"p":"tr-glp1","t":"GLP-1 Medical Weight Management — Prescriber & Support Module","g":"train","s":"Training module","k":"Prescribing doctor. Support staff have a strictly limited scope. · Doctor: institutional credentialling. Support staff: 5 supervised reviews. · GLP-1 mechanism, expected weight trajectory and the 5% Month 6 benchmark The absolute contraindications: MTC/MEN2, pancreatitis, pregnancy Titration discipline and why acceleration causes drop-out The adverse event action matrix, memorised Perioperative aspiration risk and the pre-surgery protocol Lean mass protection: why protein and resistance training are protocol, not advice Scope boundary: non-prescribing staff must NOT discuss or recommend GLP-1 independently Self-injection demonstration and teach-back Cold chain handling and temperature log completion Monthly review documentation in ERP Vitals and measurement technique"},{"p":"tr-prp","t":"PRP & GFC — Doctor Module","g":"train","s":"Training module","k":"Doctor only · Institutional credentialling · Platelet biology and the growth factors that matter Why correcting ferritin, Vitamin D and thyroid first determines the result PRP vs GFC: what actually differs Contraindications: platelet disorders, anticoagulation, active infection Managing the temporary shedding phase without losing the client Aseptic draw technique Tube labelling discipline — at the chair, in front of the client Centrifuge operation and weekly calibration check 1 cm grid injection at 4–6 mm depth FORM 21 completion"},{"p":"tr-ht","t":"Hair Transplant — Surgical Team Module","g":"train","s":"Training module","k":"Surgeon-led; technicians certified per role · Technicians: 20 supervised cases per role before independent work · Donor zone anatomy and the safe extraction boundary Graft anatomy and the determinants of survival — out-of-body time, handling, hydration Norwood and Ludwig grading Why smoking and uncontrolled diabetes reduce survival measurably Shock loss: what it is, why it happens, how to counsel it before surgery Post-operative complication recognition Punch technique and transection rate measurement Graft handling, sorting and holding solution discipline Recipient site angle, direction and density Implantation technique with minimum trauma Day 1 wash demonstration to the client"},{"p":"tr-iv","t":"IV Drip Therapy — Doctor Module","g":"train","s":"Training module","k":"Doctor only. Doctor must remain on site throughout. · Institutional credentialling · Glutathione and Vitamin C pharmacology and the off-label position on skin lightening G6PD deficiency and haemolysis risk with high-dose Vitamin C Oxalate nephropathy risk Anaphylaxis recognition and the adrenaline protocol Extravasation recognition and management Emergency kit check drill — completed before every infusion list Cannulation technique Slow-start observation discipline Anaphylaxis simulation, timed"},{"p":"tr-ems","t":"EMS / EMSculpt — Therapist Certification","g":"train","s":"Training module","k":"Therapist · Minimum 5 supervised sessions · Supramaximal contraction and muscle hypertrophy Metal, pacemaker, IUD and epilepsy contraindications Why the applicator must sit on the muscle belly Applicator positioning for abdomen, glutes, arms and thighs Intensity ramping technique Metal removal check before every session"},{"p":"tr-scalp","t":"Scalp Therapy & Hair Treatments — Therapist Certification","g":"train","s":"Training module","k":"Therapist. Mesotherapy injection is doctor only. · Minimum 8 supervised sessions · Scalp conditions: dandruff, seborrhoeic dermatitis, folliculitis, psoriasis — recognising the difference Why treating the scalp comes before treating the follicle High-frequency contraindications: pacemaker, epilepsy, broken skin Scope boundary: therapists do not inject and do not diagnose Sectioning and even product application Scalp peel application and timing High-frequency technique, direct and indirect Trichoscopy image capture at the fixed sites"},{"p":"tr-hydra","t":"HydraFacial & Medi-Facials — Therapist Certification","g":"train","s":"Training module","k":"Therapist · Minimum 8 supervised sessions · Skin typing and booster selection Recognising rosacea, active infection and contraindications Why manual cystic extraction is out of scope Tip selection and sequence Suction level adjustment by skin type Extraction technique and knowing when to stop Booster selection and the burning-vs-tingling distinction"},{"p":"tr-pmu","t":"Permanent Makeup — Artist Certification","g":"train","s":"Training module","k":"Certified PMU artist. Doctor available on site. · Minimum 20 supervised procedures · Skin layers and correct implantation depth Colour theory, undertone and how pigments heal and shift Patch testing and pigment allergy Herpes prophylaxis for lip work Bloodborne pathogen control and single-use discipline Design and symmetry with the client upright Depth control and pressure consistency Sterile setup with equipment barrier wrapping Aftercare handover"},{"p":"tr-inj","t":"Injection Lipolysis — Doctor Module","g":"train","s":"Training module","k":"Doctor only · Institutional credentialling · Lipolytic agents and their mechanism Grid mapping and depth discipline Recognising vascular compromise and tissue necrosis Why the swelling phase must be counselled before, not after Grid transfer application Perpendicular injection at 4–6 mm with swift withdrawal Post-injection pressure technique Injectable register completion"},{"p":"tr-lesion","t":"Lesion Removal & Keloid Management — Doctor Module","g":"train","s":"Training module","k":"Doctor only · Institutional credentialling · ABCDE melanoma screening and dermoscopy basics Why a pigmented lesion is never cosmetically destroyed without assessment Keloid pathophysiology and intralesional steroid protocol PIH risk in Fitzpatrick IV–VI and how to counsel it Dermoscopic assessment Controlled-depth RF cautery Specimen handling, labelling and lab dispatch logging Wound care instruction and dressing"},{"p":"tr-nutrition","t":"Clinical Nutrition & LIFE RISE — Dietician Module","g":"train","s":"Training module","k":"Qualified dietician · Minimum 10 supervised consultations · TDEE calculation and safe deficit setting Protein requirements for lean mass preservation, including on GLP-1 Indian household food composition — plans clients can actually cook Behaviour change: food logging, trigger identification, mindful eating Scope boundaries: no diagnosis, no prescription, no dose advice — and what to do instead Recognising disordered eating and the escalation pathway LIFE RISE red flags and stop rules Full first consultation with plan construction Food diary review and adjustment BCA interpretation and explaining it to a client Escalation drill: when to hand over to the doctor"},{"p":"tr-drain","t":"Lymphatic Drainage & Whole Body Therapy","g":"train","s":"Training module","k":"Therapist · Minimum 5 supervised sessions · Lymphatic anatomy and node cluster locations Why drainage determines the result of every fat-disruption treatment Contraindications: DVT, active infection, untreated cancer Manual drainage direction for upper and lower body G5/G10 technique with correct avoidance zones FDS wrap application"},{"p":"tr-bca","t":"Body Composition Analysis — Front Office & Therapist","g":"train","s":"Training module","k":"Front office or therapist · 3 supervised analyses · What bioimpedance measures and what it cannot Why standard conditions matter more than the machine Pacemaker and pregnancy contraindications Standard condition check Electrode contact and machine operation Standardised tape measurement at fixed anatomical points Photography in the fixed-lighting booth Explaining the report without alarming the client"},{"p":"tr-tricho","t":"Trichoscopy & Skin Analysis","g":"train","s":"Training module","k":"Therapist or doctor · 5 supervised assessments · Normal scalp findings vs pattern loss vs telogen effluvium Red flag findings that go to the doctor the same day Probe hygiene and cross-infection control Fixed-site imaging technique Consistent magnification and lighting mode Image labelling and storage against the client record"},{"p":"tr-nshr","t":"Non-Surgical Hair Restoration — Technician Certification","g":"train","s":"Training module","k":"NSHR technician · Minimum 10 supervised fittings · Base types, adhesives and bond life Adhesive allergy and patch testing Scalp health under a system and how to protect it Template taking Colour, density and texture matching Attachment methods and tension control Home care demonstration to the client"},{"p":"tr-wrap","t":"Inch-Loss Wraps & FDS","g":"train","s":"Training module","k":"Therapist · 3 supervised sessions · What a wrap does and does not do — fluid vs fat Allergy screening and patch testing Heat blanket contraindications Even gel application Wrap tension — the two-finger rule Heat monitoring and comfort checks"},{"p":"tr-lipolaser","t":"Lipo Laser","g":"train","s":"Training module","k":"Therapist · 3 supervised sessions · Low-level laser mechanism and why post-session activity is mandatory Contraindications per the machine IFU Pad placement and contact checking Post-session drainage or cardio handover"},{"p":"matrix","t":"Contraindications matrix","g":"ref","s":"Reference","k":"who cannot have treatment absolute relative pregnancy pacemaker metal implant isotretinoin roaccutane keloid medication screen photosensitising anticoagulant fitzpatrick"},{"p":"safety","t":"Red flags and escalation","g":"ref","s":"Reference","k":"P1 P2 P3 P4 severity emergency anaphylaxis va